// @run
// @log 7
// @log 1
// @log 7
// @hint Intentionally-unused parameter `_z` (leading `_` marks it unused)
// A `for-in` over an UNDECLARED list whose element has a nested object field matching a
// DECLARED union arm's layout — silent-class-inventory D179 (and D227, its `.slice` twin,
// which is the SAME defect once the trap was gone). Two lives, one program:
//
//   · on `a19a3db7` this was the only `compiler trap` the census grid ever produced from
//     source. `refListSlotOfExpr` CLAMPS a miss to slot `0`, `rlElemKindTbl` was empty, and
//     `forInElemKind`'s raw `rlElemKindTbl[rslot]` read killed the compiler with
//     `wasm trap: out of bounds array access` and no module written. #1972 bound-checked
//     that read and its `.slice`/`.filter` twin, which made the failure LOUD.
//   · loud is not working. The reason nothing interned the nested `{ c2: 1 }` shape is
//     `collectAnonShapes`: it skips any literal `objVariantName` claims, and that is a
//     GLOBAL scan with no context — "some union arm somewhere has this field-name set", not
//     "this literal is boxed". A nested field value of an anonymous literal is boxed by
//     nothing, so the skip left the shape un-interned and the whole program with no
//     ref-list row at all: `emitProgram: ref valtype with no interned shape`.
//
// The three reads below are the three spellings that reached the two trap sites: the
// `for-in` binding (D179's), `.slice(…)[0]` (D227's, which trapped INSIDE
// `refListElemNameOfExpr` where no caller's guard could reach) and `.filter`. All three
// now build the same interned shapes and run.
//
// One-line ablations, all built and run: delete the three `type` lines and it RUNS on every
// compiler; declare `Cir2` without making it a union member and it RUNS; give the nested
// literal a field name no arm has (`{ zz9: 1 }`) and it RUNS. What the defect needed was the
// declared-arm layout at a nested field of an un-annotated literal.
//
// THE CONDITION THAT MAKES THE ROW SAFE is in `anon-nested-arm-layout-polymorphic.vl`: two
// owner literals that share a field-name set but disagree about the nested layout must KEEP
// the union box, because `structIndexOfObj` would match them to one row.
type Cir2 = { c2: i32 }
type Sq2 = { s2: i32 }
type Shape2 = Cir2 | Sq2
const c = [{ r: { c2: 1 } }]
let hit = 0
for zz in c {
  if zz.r is Cir2 { hit = 7 }
}
print(hit)
print(c.slice(0, 1)[0].r.c2)
const d = c.filter((_z) => true)
print(d[0].r.c2 + 6)
